  "final_verdict": "MIXED_NO_CLEAN_UNIVERSALITY",
  "headline": "Amplitude-normalized 2-term model FAILS TO SUPPORT the regime-transition hypothesis. Neither d1 (secondary coefficient) nor d2 (error coefficient) is prime-independent within either family. d1 imag flips sign at p=13 (same numerical issue as R18o). d1 real monotone grows in magnitude with p (-0.6 \u2192 -4.3) but is not constant. Per-prime fits have R\u00b2>0.97, so the model fits each prime well \u2014 but the coefficients themselves vary with p in a way not captured by \"regime transition\" alone.",
  "final_interpretation": {
    "regime_transition_hypothesis": "PARTIALLY SUPPORTED / NOT CLEANLY CONFIRMED",
    "what_we_learned": [
      "The 2-term structure (X^(-1/6) + X^(-1/3)) can fit each prime's bucket data with R\u00b2>0.97 \u2014 the exponents are plausible.",
      "The COEFFICIENTS (d1, d2) vary with prime in ways that go beyond the simple \"secondary dominates at p=3, error dominates at large p\" picture.",
      "With only 4 buckets per prime, the 2-parameter fit is underdetermined \u2014 we cannot distinguish \"hypothesis false\" from \"hypothesis true but data insufficient.\""
    ],
    "cascade_closing_remark": "The BST-literature-confirmed 1/6 rate at p=3 (Pattern-5 reproduction) is the strongest result of the cascade. Extending it to a universal regime-transition theory at larger primes requires either (a) more LMFDB buckets (not available; we are at cutoff), (b) theoretical BST coefficient values to constrain the fit, or (c) a completely different model. We stop here.",
    "no_further_actionable_tracks": "Model C (amplitude-normalized) was the last cheap empirical test. Its mixed result indicates data-limited territory. Further progress needs literature-level access or different data sources."
  }
